## Development vs Production

### Development (.env.local)
```env
NODE_ENV=development
LOG_LEVEL=debug
DEBUG=true
JWT_SECRET=dev-secret-key-not-secure
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:password@localhost:5432/kyndex_dev
FRONTEND_URL=http://localhost:3001
```

### Staging (.env.staging)
```env
NODE_ENV=staging
LOG_LEVEL=info
JWT_SECRET=${VAULT_JWT_SECRET}
DATABASE_URL=${VAULT_DATABASE_URL}
FRONTEND_URL=https://staging.kyndex.com
```

### Production (.env.production)
```env
NODE_ENV=production
LOG_LEVEL=warn
JWT_SECRET=${VAULT_JWT_SECRET}
DATABASE_URL=${VAULT_DATABASE_URL}
FRONTEND_URL=https://kyndex.com
SENTRY_ENABLED=true
MONITOR_ENABLED=true
```

---

## Setting Up Environment Files

### Step 1: Copy Template
```bash
# Backend
cp .env.example .env.local

# Frontend
cd frontend
cp .env.example .env.local
```

### Step 2: Fill In Values
Edit `.env.local` with your configuration values.

### Step 3: Verify
```bash
# Backend
npm run env:check

# Frontend (Next.js validates on startup)
npm run dev
```

---

## Security Best Practices

### ❌ DON'T
- Never commit `.env` files to Git
- Never use same secrets for different environments
- Never log sensitive values

### ✅ DO
- Use `.env.example` as template with placeholder values
- Rotate secrets regularly
- Use secret management tools (Vault, AWS Secrets Manager)
- Use strong random keys for JWT_SECRET

### Generate Strong Keys
```bash
# JWT Secret (Node.js)
node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"

# Or use openssl
openssl rand -hex 32
```

